  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 469(g)(1), when a taxpayer completely disposes of their entire interest in a passive activity in a fully taxable transaction to an unrelated party, all suspended passive losses are released and deductible in full against any type of income — active, portfolio, or passive. This is the primary mechanism by which suspended losses become fully deductible.
Wrong (B): Suspended losses are not permanently lost; the complete disposition rule under Section 469(g) specifically provides for their full deduction.
Wrong (C): Released passive losses are not converted to capital losses; they are deducted as ordinary losses against any income category.
Wrong (D): Suspended losses do not remain suspended after a complete taxable disposition; that event triggers their immediate release.$EXPL$
